Then you go find him. Right? Like let's go

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.240
Cancer crew what's up?

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.610
So, I mean apart from the fact that dating in general is very difficult,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.569
it's like: How am I gonna find someone who- A: will not think of it that way

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.669
But then he also has to deal with me thinking that I am that way

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:06.059
I guess I just never really- got- been into relationships

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.360
I guess really the only light kind of hamper with that is be like: "Yo,

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:15.178
I've had cancer, like this could come back, like I- and also like- I can't have kids anymore" right?

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.500
So like now I have some new hurdles that somebody now has to deal with, with me

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.460
I'm actually going to change my answer after hearing you, because I'm in the exact same boat

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.760
Im in Frodo as well, like I can't have children as a result of both being testicular cancer survivors.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.520
I often think about what...

00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:36.629
Sorry, I don´t...

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:38.629
I would think about what,

00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:43.199
what it's gonna be like now when I, you know, find someone when I meet a,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.530
a great woman, and now I can't-

00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:50.009
You know, maybe have children if she would want children

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.159
How would I bring it up in a conversation? What I ever?

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:58.858
I think that, like when someone falls in love with you, they're gonna fall in love with you and-

00:02:58.858 --> 00:03:00.069
um-

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:03.929
hearing that isn't gonna change it, and if it does

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:07.320
they're probably not the person that you should be with.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.349
I was able to find someone really amazing. Who's

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.650
like stuck through all this with me, and like, I was bald on tinder being like: "Who wants this?"

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.840
It's still really hard, like it did make it difficult to help these relationships grow.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.600
Im also like, processing everything right now,

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.780
while like, formulating this relationship with someone. So it's like a little bit of a struggle.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.520
My diagnosis affected my relationship with my family.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.040
Three, two, one- go.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.960
So, it did affect my relationship with my family, but for the better, so we became a lot closer-

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.199
so we went through it as a, as a family.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.450
I was lucky enough to have the right friends around me and the support, and I'm pretty sure without them

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.200
I wouldn't be standing here today, for sure.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.600
I kind of had a little bit of a different experience.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.120
I like pushed everybody away

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.740
Oh God, okay.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.500
I like pushed everybody away through out my treatment and everything.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.140
It's also kind of new, because it just happened about a year ago

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:26.009
I'm from Texas and so my mom like, moved- moved out here to come live with- live with me during my treatment

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.200
and I'm having like a really hard time connecting with her because

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.870
I keep like associating her,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.190
with that time.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.720
So...

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.800
-I've also never said that out loud. So, sorry I'm getting super emotional right now, but... -Don't apologize. -Don't be sorry.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.860
yeah, so...

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.200
I feel like there's this protective barrier on both sides and having been a survivor and

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.789
the loved one of someone who had cancer.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.549
it's: "I want to protect you guys from

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.860
thinking I'm not gonna make it , and from being hurt and from being sad."

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.600
And they want to protect me from thinking they're worried or

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.629
God forbid I would lose hope or anything of that

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:11.220
And so we think we're protecting each other, but we're really creating a disconnect

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.230
and I feel like it's long long after

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:14.700
We survive...

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:23.720
that disconnect is there, we don't go back and become transparent about what we need to do to really move forward in life.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:24.220
Yeah...

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.960
There was a moment when I thought I wouldn't survive.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.659
Three, two , one go.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.929
I mean, I think initially with any cancer, you always think that, you know,

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.920
you're not gonna survive very early on.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.550
I was not told, you know, what the prognosis was, "Was I gonna be okay?"

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:57.569
"What's my survival percentage?" And so, there was one very, very dark moment for me.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.469
I tried to take my own life, because I thought

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.770
that, that was going to be my life from here on out and

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:09.720
I would recommend right now, to anyone who is a cancer patient,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.589
please don't do anything like that- don't,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.710
because I did not have all the information yet.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.759
I did not know what was going to happen or what wasn't.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.000
Can I hug you?

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:25.120
Yeah!

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:28.950
And I think for me it's the same thing,

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:35.729
it was that dark place in between diagnosis and not knowing. And it was that, I just remember laying in bed thinking: "Shit,

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:42.420
am I gonna die? Is this it? Like, I need to take cooking classes in Italy. I haven't seen Niagara Falls

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.300
I haven't done the stuff I want to do."

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:51.160
When they diagnosed me in the name of the stage, it's 2B Unfavor able

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.660
and I'm like first of all, why would you do that to me? Like, why would you put unfavorable in the name?

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.000
That's just rude.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.840
She explained to me that, you know, it was just a formality just a name, like the treatment you...

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:06.000
should be responding to it really well and so going through it

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.530
I was like: "Okay, you know what? I can do this."

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:11.940
But then there got to a point in the chemo that I was like: "This, is what I might not survive"

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.220
Like the cancer, okay

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.270
I was living in with it for like maybe, I don't know, like a year? And I seemed to be doing fine then, man

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.170
but this chemo is

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:22.469
really making me suffer.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:26.660
I have experienced survivor's guilt.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.640
Three, two, one, go.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.380
I could have been born poor,

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.320
I could have had a different kind of cancer, like all of these different things

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.770
working together, and I'm not saying that

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:53.009
I shouldn't have survived, and I'm not saying that I didn't work very hard and my doctors didn't work very hard.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:08:00.990
But on some level, I do feel guilt because there are so many people who...

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:07.469
deserve to have survived and haven't, and why is it that I was chosen to be one of those people?

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:11.130
In my second year of treatment, I would have been ten years old,

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:19.260
the hospital that I went to had this art show where all the kids get to paint and- and make some cool drawings and I

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.280
saw next door to me,

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.559
there was another photo but no one was there and reality kind of sank in, and I asked my dad

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.869
I was like: "Dad, why isn't he here?" and later that night

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:31.500
he said: "I don't think he made it." And so I think about that kid every day.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:39.119
I feel bad that I don't remember his name, but I think about his family, his mom who doesn't have a son anymore and

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.620
he's never gonna get to go to

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:42.880
prom and

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:49.080
graduate college and get the job of his dreams and have a family. And it's, that, that one was was pretty tough for me

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:50.640
When I lost my brother

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:56.400
he was 32 years old , to cancer, but I don't know why his journey in this life was only 32 years.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:09:01.109
But I know he did impress a lot of things and a lot of people in that 32 years.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:02.650
I believe...

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:09.239
that if my brother could see me and I struggled for a very long time after losing him, that he would have said:

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:11.759
"The last thing I ever want to see is you being sad.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:17.729
Enjoy your life. You don't have to be Wonder Woman. Like, you just have to live your best life, your happiest life.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:21.120
And maybe it's enjoying pizza and laughing with your friends,

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:27.520
And that's your best life, have it and love it, because it's short and- and don't, don't miss out on it."

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.080
I am afraid of getting cancer again.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.660
Three

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:32.800
Two

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:33.600
One

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:34.460
Go

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:39.760
So, I'm the weirdo?

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:40.860
Alright, cool.

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:45.060
So, there's a slang term within the cancer world known as scan xiety,

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:45.730
and you know

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:49.210
it's when you go in for a scan, you get inside and you're like: "I don't know if cancer is gonna come back now,"

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:52.920
or whatever like, I can't change it. There was literally nothing I can do

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:56.300
doesn't matter how I eat, doesn't matter what I do, doesn't matter what people tell you.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.560
There's lots of vegans, they get Cancer

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.620
doesn't freaking matter. So like,

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:04.100
I just have to beat it again. You know, what else am I gonna do?

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.920
God. You're such a positive person.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:09.540
I wanna hang out with you.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.960
I think it is just because, I like just finished my treatment and everything.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:15.200
And so I think that's why

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.809
It's so scary, because like I haven't even been cancer-free for like a year yet or anything like that

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.810
you know, it's just a lot of

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:28.840
anxiety, but I think like with time, you know, kind of like you guys are saying that: "It's still kind of scary,

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.649
but you'll slowly make your way over there." I think that'll happen with me too so,

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.380
Surviving cancer has made me a stronger person.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.540
Three, two, one, go.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:55.260
I mean not to toot my own horn, but I would say that I would have had to have been a strong person

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.290
before.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.290
I feel like surviving cancer made me

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:04.320
realize that I was a strong person, but I don't think it made me stronger per se.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.800
I mean I was a strong person and a passionate person my entire life

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:14.830
but I think it made me even stronger, even less tolerant of pettiness, of drama

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:22.800
you know, when that kind of stuff comes up, it was like no, I don't have time for that, and it made me very

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:28.240
assertive in the fact that, if I want to accomplish this, I'm in it right now.

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:34.140
I like to think as well that I was strong before, but it's only after you experience something so

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:41.679
life-changing that you realize how truly strong you are, you know, it's no longer what I think it's how I think...

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:48.620
that really puts a smile on my face. And so in that regard, it has made me very strong.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.720
It's very good to say that, I like that.

